{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1070","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1070","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1070\",\"parent_revision\":\"4d934a56f80583400416d34f19cc28bc2b3b8d96\",\"root_revision\":\"ff4916314bb99bf92a3c645ea36a6583dacb2b19bf5f836a325ae90887200a4c\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nCompanion to #1069. The compaction recovery path in #1069 fires at 100% context (PreCompact). This item adds an earlier warning: when context pressure reaches a configurable threshold (default 40%), inject a structured prompt asking the orchestrator to flush its current team state to beads before compaction is forced.\\n\\nMechanism: A UserPromptSubmit or PostToolUse hook estimates context pressure by checking the transcript file size or token count from the transcript path available in all hook inputs. When the estimate crosses the threshold, the hook outputs a prompt (via hookSpecificOutput or stdout) asking the orchestrator to run `bd update <task> --notes \\\"Active teams: ...\\\"`.\\n\\nOpen questions:\\n1. Does any hook input field expose current token usage directly? (transcript_path is available \u2014 can read and estimate from file size)\\n2. Is UserPromptSubmit or PostToolUse the better firing point? (UserPromptSubmit fires less frequently)\\n3. Should the threshold be per-project config (bd config set context-flush-threshold 40) or a dh plugin setting?\\n\\nThis is separate from #1069 because it targets early warning, not recovery. #1069 must ship first.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-25T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-25T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **feat: proactive context-pressure handoff flush at configurable threshold** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Design session 2026-03-25: extension of #1069 compaction recovery work\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-25\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-03-25)\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- Context pressure is measurable from the transcript file (JSONL) available at `transcript_path` in hook input\\n- At the configured threshold (default 40%), the hook injects a prompt asking the orchestrator to flush team state to beads\\n- Threshold is configurable (e.g., `bd config set context-flush-threshold 40`)\\n- The flush prompt is injected at most once per compaction cycle \u2014 not on every tool call after threshold is crossed\\n- If the threshold cannot be measured reliably (e.g., transcript not readable), the hook is a no-op\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n- **Context pressure measurement**: No hook event fires at a pressure threshold. The transcript_path is available in PostToolUse/UserPromptSubmit \u2014 need to verify that file size / message count is a reliable proxy for token usage. May not be accurate enough to be useful.\\n- **Hook frequency**: PostToolUse fires on every tool call. Even with deduplication, this adds overhead. UserPromptSubmit fires less often but only when the user sends a message \u2014 the orchestrator may not send user messages during long autonomous runs.\\n- **Blocked on #1069**: The flush prompt is only useful if #1069 already ensures there is something meaningful in beads to flush to. If beads has no team state, the prompt fires but accomplishes nothing.\\n- **May be unnecessary**: If #1069 writes team state to beads incrementally (after every TeamCreate and TeammateIdle), the PreCompact hook already has fresh state to inject. The proactive flush at 40% adds redundancy but may not add correctness.\\n\\n**Recommendation**: Validate whether #1069 alone solves the problem in practice before investing in the threshold mechanism. Demote to P2/Ideas if #1069 proves sufficient.\\n\",\"digest\":\"3ceb5bb0a8bc8979a799210b9ffa97e87d7837b4cac7cf918e332229a2ea3bcf\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPg6dfg\"}"}